    </li>
    </ul>
    <p>To combat these issues, some residents opt for <strong>weather-resistant mailboxes</strong>. Galvanized or powder-coated metal boxes resist rust better, and <strong>vinyl or composite posts</strong> don’t rot like wood. If your mailbox has been suffering yearly weather-related damage, upgrading to a more durable material (or applying a fresh coat of waterproof paint each year) can save money long-term. For example, repainting a metal mailbox with quality enamel paint and primer before winter (a DIY task that might cost $20-$30 in supplies) can stave off rust and postpone the need for a replacement.</p>
